5187,2555,92,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the matrix $\\mathbf{M},$ with real entries, such that\n\\[\\mathbf{M}^3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 19 & 30 \\\\ -45 & -71 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"51b4f60dc4062ba943e9cb9e4a9b37c8358cf469e70b61a6a22e965bd18837a6"},"seconds":77.07053685188293,"seed":131909150754989474,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) such that \\(\\mathbf{M}^3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 19 & 30 \\\\ -45 & -71 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to determine a matrix whose cube is the given matrix. Let's denote the given matrix by \\(\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 19 & 30 \\\\ -45 & -71 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). The characteristic polynomial of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is given by the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}\\):\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 19 - \\lambda & 30 \\\\ -45 & -71 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = (19 - \\lambda)(-71 - \\lambda) - (-45)(30).\n\\]\nCalculating the determinant, we get:\n\\[\n(19 - \\lambda)(-71 - \\lambda) + 1350 = \\lambda^2 + 52\\lambda + 19 \\cdot (-71) + 1350 = \\lambda^2 + 52\\lambda - 1349 + 1350 = \\lambda^2 + 52\\lambda + 1.\n\\]\nThe eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic polynomial \\(\\lambda^2 + 52\\lambda + 1 = 0\\). Using the quadratic formula \\(\\lambda = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{-52 \\pm \\sqrt{52^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-52 \\pm \\sqrt{2704 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-52 \\pm \\sqrt{2700}}{2} = \\frac{-52 \\pm 30\\sqrt{3}}{2} = -26 \\pm 15\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\nSo, the eigenvalues are \\(\\lambda_1 = -26 + 15\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(\\lambda_2 = -26 - 15\\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nSince the eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) are \\(\\lambda_1^3\\) and \\(\\lambda_2^3\\) and we need \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) such that \\(\\mathbf{M}^3 = \\mathbf{A}\\), the eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) should be the cube roots of \\(\\lambda_1\\) and \\(\\lambda_2\\). Let's denote the eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) by \\(\\mu_1\\) and \\(\\mu_2\\) such that \\(\\mu_1^3 = \\lambda_1\\) and \\(\\mu_2^3 = \\lambda_2\\). One possible choice is \\(\\mu_1 = -2 + \\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(\\mu_2 = -2 - \\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nNow, we need to find a matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) with eigenvalues \\(-2 + \\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(-2 - \\sqrt{3}\\). A matrix with these eigenvalues and similar form to \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe can verify that \\(\\mathbf{M}^3 = \\mathbf{A}\\) by direct computation:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 3 & -2\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 6 - 6 & -3\\sqrt{3} + 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M}^3 = \\mathbf{M} \\mathbf{M}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 8\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3 & 12\\sqrt{3} - 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 19 & 30 \\\\ -45 & -71 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
